Who&When Pro Small-Scale Reproduction
=====================================

A small-scale reproduction of the data generation and evaluation pipeline
from "Who&When Pro: Can LLMs Really Attribute Failures in AI Agents?"
(Liu et al., arXiv:2607.09996). It builds labeled agent-failure traces by
injecting a single error into an agent's own successful runs, then measures
how well LLM judges can find the step that broke the run and name what went
wrong.

The full benchmark spans 12,326 traces across 26 benchmarks and 15 agent
frameworks. I rebuilt the whole pipeline end to end at small scale, on one
framework and one benchmark: a smolagents CodeAgent answering DA-Bench
data-analysis questions. I generated 34 seed runs, turned them into 14
labeled failure traces, evaluated those with the paper's own harness, and
compared the results against the paper's released traces for the same
setting. The whole thing cost about $6.50 in API calls.

The idea being reproduced
-------------------------

Failure attribution asks: given a failed agent run, which step was the
decisive error, the earliest step that, if fixed alone, would have turned
the failure into a success (the definition from the original Who&When
paper, Zhang et al., ICML 2025). Labeling this by hand is slow and
unreliable, so Who&When Pro generates labels by construction.

Start from a run that succeeded. Pick a step t, replay steps 1 to t-1
exactly as they happened, swap in a corrupted action at step t, and let the
agent continue on its own. If the run now fails, t is the decisive step:
undoing the change gives back the original success. The key improvement
over earlier injection methods is the exact replay. Those methods rerun the
task from scratch, so the agent can drift before the injection point and
the label is only approximate.

The corrupted step is built in two stages. A stronger "frontier" model
reads the task, the correct answer, and what the agent has seen so far, and
describes a specific, plausible mistake. That description is then added to
the base agent model's context for that one step, so the agent writes the
mistake itself, in its own style.

Scope
-----

* Framework: smolagents, single-agent CodeAgent. It is the largest share of
  the paper's text traces, has the simplest structure (no multi-agent
  orchestration), and its warm-start replay is documented in the paper
  (Appendix F.3).
* Benchmark: DA-Bench (InfiAgent-DABench). Each question comes with a local
  CSV and is answered by writing Python, so there are no live web calls and
  grading is exact-match with the official evaluator.
* Error modes: R.2 (reasoning error) and R.3 (calculation error).
* Models: Claude Haiku 4.5 as the base agent, Claude Sonnet 5 as the
  frontier model for injection, and Claude Sonnet 4.6 and Claude Haiku 4.5
  as judges.

I chose the error modes from the paper's failure-mode profile for this
setting: across the 327 smolagents x DA-Bench traces the paper released,
the modes are A.3 (135), R.4 (60), R.2 (55), A.2 (40), R.3 (37), and R.1
(0). R.1 (hallucination) has a published prompt template, but it is built
around web search and never occurs on DA-Bench, so I left it out. R.3 has
no published template, so I wrote my own (see Error injection).

Haiku 4.5 was chosen to fit a $5 starting budget. Like the paper's
GPT-4.1, it writes its reasoning into the visible output the judge reads
rather than hiding it. Sonnet 4.6 is one of the judges the paper
evaluates, which gives a direct comparison.


Pipeline
========

Seed generation
---------------

Code: repro/dabench.py, repro/grader.py, repro/claude_model.py,
repro/agent.py, scripts/run_seeds.py. Output: outputs/seeds/.

The agent's task prompt is not my own. All 327 of the paper's released
smolagents x DA-Bench traces start from the same fixed template, so I
recovered it and filled it from the official DA-Bench files. My prompts
match the released ones character for character, 327 of 327.

Answers are graded by DA-Bench's official evaluator (eval_closed_form.py),
copied unmodified into da-bench/ and pinned to upstream commit 0bf51d9.
Grading is strict: the answer must use the required @name[value] format,
and a question only passes if every sub-answer is correct. I considered
filtering out injected runs that fail only on formatting, since those
arguably fail for a different reason than the injected error, but the
paper does not do this, so I kept the official evaluator as the only
pass/fail rule.

I ran the agent on 40 questions drawn from the 163 DA-Bench questions the
paper's traces use, balanced across difficulty (14 easy, 13 medium, 13
hard) and picked with a fixed random seed. The agent gets at most 15 steps
and can import only the libraries the paper's own traces used (pandas,
numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, statsmodels, statistics, re, datetime).
Every step's reasoning, code, output, and error is recorded, which is what
the replay stage needs.

36 of 40 runs were correct. The four wrong ones look like the paper's
error types: a missing bracket in the answer format (A.3), a standard
deviation that divided by n instead of n-1 (R.3), a small rounding
difference, and a gold answer that contains a file path from the original
benchmark environment, which no agent could reproduce. A seed must be
correct with at least 2 steps. I excluded two more by hand: q321 used all
15 steps, leaving no room for an injected run to continue, and q590 cannot
be replayed exactly (below). That leaves 34 seeds.

Warm-start replay
-----------------

Code: repro/replay_model.py, scripts/check_replay.py.

The paper replays smolagents runs with a SQLite cache keyed by a SHA-256
hash of each tool call's parameters, so that web searches return the same
results as the original run. I did not build this cache, because my agent
makes no external tool calls. Its only action is running Python on a local
CSV, which is a stateful environment: later steps depend on variables
earlier steps created. The paper handles stateful environments by
re-executing the recorded actions with a fidelity check, and that is what
I implemented.

smolagents talks to its model through a single "give me the next reply"
function. I wrote a replay model that answers the first t-1 of those calls
with the replies recorded in the seed run instead of calling the LLM.
smolagents parses and runs that code exactly as it would a live reply,
which rebuilds both the conversation and the Python state. From step t
onward, calls go to the live model. After each replayed step, the output is
compared byte for byte with the recording, and any difference aborts the
attempt, as in the paper.

Replaying every step of every seed with no API calls reproduces 34 of the
35 correct seeds exactly. The exception, q590, hit an import error whose
message lists the allowed imports in set order, which changes with Python's
hash randomization. That message is part of the agent's context, so the
state before any later step cannot be reproduced exactly, and I dropped the
seed.

Error injection
---------------

Code: repro/injection.py, scripts/build_eligibility_prompt.py,
scripts/pick_injection_steps.py, scripts/run_injection.py, prompts/.
Output: outputs/injection/.

Choosing the step. The paper samples t conditioned on the failure mode,
skipping steps with execution errors and steps where that mode could not
naturally occur, but does not publish the exact rules. Its released
DA-Bench traces never inject at step 1 or at the final-answer step, and
almost always at steps 2 to 5. My candidates are steps 2 to T-1 without
errors (77 steps across 34 seeds). A prompt I wrote
(prompts/step_eligibility.txt) then asks, for each candidate and mode,
whether that kind of mistake could naturally happen there and change the
answer: R.3 needs a step that computes a number the answer depends on, R.2
needs a step that makes a choice or interpretation. It also excludes steps
where the full correct answer was already printed. Claude Sonnet 5 on
claude.ai declined this prompt, so the judgments were made by Claude Opus
5.5 inside a Claude Code session; the prompt and the response are both
saved. Step t is then drawn at random from each eligible list with a fixed
seed, giving 64 attempts (33 R.2, 31 R.3).

Stage 1, the flaw. Sonnet 5 gets the task, the correct answer, the outputs
of steps 1 to t-1, and the seed's original step t, and returns a FLAW (a
specific mistake) and the WRONG_ANSWER it would lead to. For R.2 this is
the paper's Appendix K.2 prompt, word for word including its web-flavored
examples. For R.3 I wrote a template that mirrors the R.2 one line for
line, with the paper's R.3 definition and made-up data-analysis examples
(n vs n-1, the wrong denominator, rounding too early). It draws the
boundary as "right data, right method, wrong computation"; misreading which
data or column to use counts as R.2.

Stage 2, the corrupted step. At step t, the stage 2 prompt, built around
the FLAW, is appended to Haiku's normal context for that one call, and
Haiku writes the step. The prompt is never stored in the agent's memory, so
later steps and the final trace contain only Haiku's own reply. I changed
one line of the paper's R.2 stage 2 prompt: it tells the agent to call a
web-search tool, and my agent has none, so it now tells the agent to write
analysis code instead, still without calling final_answer.

A conceptual problem with R.3 in a code-writing agent: Python does the
arithmetic, so the agent cannot really "add wrong." Calculation errors show
up as wrong formulas in code (ddof, rounding, denominators), which sits
very close to R.2's "local derivation bug." I kept the R.3 label for these
and treat the ambiguity as a finding (see Results).

Pilots. I ran the same four attempts three times before the full run, and
kept the first two pilots in outputs/injection/pilot1 and pilot2.

    Pilot  Changes before the pilot                    Mistake made  Failed
    1      paper's prompts, R.2 web line swapped        1 of 4        0 of 4
    2      stage 1 also sees the seed's step t; R.2     2 of 4        1 of 4
           stage 2 requires the code to act on it
    3      stage 1: the flaw must change what this      3 of 4        1 of 4
           step computes or prints

In pilot 1, Sonnet only saw the outputs before step t, not the step
itself. It guessed wrong about which std formula the gold answer used and
"injected" the correct one, and it planned a mistake for a test that only
ran a step later. Haiku also ignored an instruction to use the wrong
column. The fixes are departures from the paper's published templates, but
they follow the paper's description that the frontier model reads the
seed's context up to step t. After pilot 3, the remaining losses were
mostly Haiku double-checking its own work, not prompt problems.

Filtering and export
--------------------

Code: scripts/export_eval_data.py. Output: outputs/eval_data/.

Of the 64 attempts, 14 ended in a wrong answer. The paper keeps only failed
runs and removes two kinds of trace (section 3.3): ones where the injected
step leaks the construction (for example, mentions being told to make a
mistake), and ones where the correct answer was already visible before
step t. The leak check scans the injected step for phrases like "inject",
"instruct", "flaw", and "deliberate"; I also read all 14 injected steps by
hand. The salience check looks for every gold value in the earlier
outputs, and skips answers shorter than three characters, since values
like "4" match unrelated numbers in any printed table (one trace, q514, is
flagged as unchecked). All 14 traces pass. None failed on formatting only,
hit the step limit, or had the injected step call final_answer.

Some injected steps contain comments that contradict the code. In q496, a
comment saying the STEM feature is created as a sum sits right above a
line that takes the mean; q376, q514, and q716 have similar tells. They do
not mention the injection, so they pass the paper's rule, but they point
straight at the bad step.

The kept traces are written in the paper's release format (one JSON row
per trace with the task, trajectory, and ground truth) so the paper's
evaluation harness reads them unchanged. The judge sees only the question
and the trajectory; the flaw text and filter decisions live in a separate
manifest.json that is never shown to it.

Evaluation
----------

Code: whowhen_eval/ (the paper's harness, unmodified).
Output: outputs/eval_results/, outputs/control_data/,
outputs/control_results/.

The judge reads the whole failed trace and names the agent, the decisive
step, and the error mode from the full 17-mode taxonomy (the all-at-once
protocol, the paper's primary one). Scoring is the harness's own: step
accuracy, error-mode macro-F1 (the paper's Error column), and joint
accuracy. Agent accuracy does not apply to a single-agent framework. Both
judges ran with the harness defaults (temperature 0, 2048 output tokens).

As a control, I also ran Sonnet 4.6 on the paper's own released traces for
exactly this setting: all 92 smolagents x DA-Bench traces labeled R.2 (55)
or R.3 (37). This shows whether my traces are as hard to attribute as the
paper's, without the paper's cross-benchmark averaging getting in the way.


Results
=======

Injection yield
---------------

14 of 64 attempts (22%) produced a usable failure, 6 of 33 for R.2 (18%)
and 8 of 31 for R.3 (26%). The rest recovered. By injection step: t=2 gave
5 of 32, t=3 gave 8 of 28, t=4 gave 1 of 3, and t=5 gave 0 of 1. Sonnet 5
never refused a stage 1 prompt.

Judge accuracy
--------------

Judges on my 14 traces, next to the paper's Table 4 row for Sonnet 4.6
(averaged over all text benchmarks):

    Judge                    Step    Error F1   Joint   Mode acc.
    Sonnet 4.6 (mine)        85.7%   45.5       28.6%   5/14
    Haiku 4.5 (mine)         92.9%   37.8       35.7%   5/14
    Sonnet 4.6 (paper)       69.8%   19.1       22.4%   -

Haiku and Sonnet differ by only 1-2 traces, which is noise at this size.
Haiku judging traces from its own model did not visibly hurt it. With 14
traces each one is about 7 percentage points, so my own numbers should be
read as "consistent with" or "higher than" the paper, not as precise
estimates.

Comparison with the paper's traces
----------------------------------

Same judge (Sonnet 4.6), same setting, my traces against the paper's:

                             Mine (14)   Paper's (92)
    Step accuracy            85.7%       46.7%
    Error-mode accuracy      35.7%       20.7%
    Error macro-F1           45.5        23.3
    Joint                    28.6%       14.1%
    Mean length / mean t     4.7 / 2.7   5.4 / 3.6
    Random-step baseline     22.3%       20.9%

My traces are much easier to localize than the paper's. The step accuracy
gap is significant even with 14 traces (Fisher's exact test, p = 0.009);
the error-mode gap is not (p = 0.30). Trace length does not explain it: on
traces of 4 steps or fewer the judge scored 100% on mine against 67% on the
paper's, and on 5-6 step traces 78% against 36%. The likely causes are that
my injected errors are visible slips in the code, some with contradicting
comments; that my injection points are earlier; and that Haiku is a
different base agent than the paper's GPT-4.1 and Gemini 3 Flash.

Error modes
-----------

Error-mode classification is weak on both sets, which reproduces the
paper's central finding. R.3 is the hardest: Sonnet named it correctly on 2
of my 8 R.3 traces and on 1 of the paper's 37, calling most of them R.4 or
R.2. Since this happens on the paper's own data, it is not an artifact of
my R.3 template; it looks like a property of the taxonomy for agents whose
arithmetic is done by code. It matches a trace I annotated by hand before
building anything (dabench_smolagents_0001), where the agent picked vote
counts instead of the visible percentage columns: I read that as R.2, while
the official label is A.3 (output format).


Discussion
==========

Where this differs from the paper
---------------------------------

* Base agent: Claude Haiku 4.5 instead of GPT-4.1 / Gemini 3 Flash.
* Replay: re-execution with byte-level fidelity checks; no SHA-256 tool
  cache, since the agent makes no external tool calls.
* Step selection: my own eligibility prompt, judged by Claude Opus 5.5.
* Prompts: the R.2 web-search instruction replaced with analysis code; my
  own R.3 templates; stage 1 also sees the original step t; the flaw must
  change the step's code or output; R.2 stage 2 requires the code to act on
  the flaw.
* No retries: the paper retries a failed attempt at another step; I made
  one attempt per seed and mode and report the yield.
* Salience check: skips gold values shorter than three characters.
* Scale: 14 traces, one framework, one benchmark, two modes.
* Taxonomy: the released taxonomy.yaml has 17 modes, while the paper
  describes 18.

Limitations
-----------

The sample is small, so most per-mode comparisons are anecdotal. The mode
labels are the injected ones, as in the paper, but several of my traces
arguably belong to a different mode (the two q666 traces make the same ddof
mistake under different labels). Step labels are decisive by construction
only if the failure comes from the injection; I did not run the check that
replays to step t without injecting anything, which would measure how often
the agent fails on its own after a warm start. The eligibility judgments
were made by a single model without a second annotator.


Running it
==========

Everything runs from the repository root with the project's Python:

    python3.13 -m venv .venv
    .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

The DA-Bench data goes in da-bench/data/ (see da-bench/SOURCE.md) and the
Who&When Pro release in whowhen-pro/ (Hugging Face: Leoxx/whowhen_pro).
API keys go in .env (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).

Stage 1, seed generation, and the stage 2 replay check:

    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.run_seeds --n 40
    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.check_replay

Stage 3, error injection:

    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.build_eligibility_prompt
    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.pick_injection_steps
    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.run_injection

Stage 4, filtering and export:

    .venv/bin/python -m scripts.export_eval_data

Stage 5, evaluation:

    .venv/bin/python -m whowhen_eval.run --model claude-sonnet-4-6 \
        --data-root outputs/eval_data --modality text \
        --out outputs/eval_results
    .venv/bin/python -m whowhen_eval.leaderboard \
        --results outputs/eval_results

All outputs, including every run, prompt, and model reply, are committed
under outputs/, since the LLM runs cannot be regenerated identically.
Finished runs are skipped on rerun, so nothing is paid for twice.


